# =============================================================================
#  PUBLIC-REPO SAFETY. Read PUBLISHING.md before relaxing anything here.
#  Two categories below are NOT ordinary "big/generated file" ignores:
#    (1) the private eval content  -- publishing it DESTROYS its value
#    (2) private working logs      -- session history, not project content
# =============================================================================

# ---- (1) PRIVATE EVAL CONTENT -- NEVER COMMIT --------------------------------
# The whole point of these suites is that they exist nowhere public, so no model
# has trained on them. Decontaminated SWE-rebench scores A3B-class models ~4x
# below their self-reported SWE-bench numbers -- that gap IS benchmark leakage.
# Committing these turns a clean measurement into another contaminated one, for
# every model released after the push date. The HARNESS is public; the ANSWERS
# are not. See PUBLISHING.md for how to author your own.
evals/tools/cases.jsonl
evals/code/tasks.json
evals/code/tests/
evals/code/reference/

# ---- (2) PRIVATE WORKING LOGS ------------------------------------------------
.remember/

# ---- (3) NESTED PRIVATE REPO -------------------------------------------------
# ornith-router is its OWN git repo with a remote on the internal GitLab (the URL
# includes an internal group/org path). Two reasons it is excluded here:
#   - as a bare gitlink with no .gitmodules it would clone as an EMPTY directory
#     in a public repo -- broken for anyone who clones;
#   - wiring it as a real submodule would publish that internal remote URL.
# Keep it as its own internal repo. To fold it in for real, delete its .git and
# un-ignore this line -- but re-run the secret scan afterwards, because its
# history and config reference internal infrastructure.
ornith-router/

# ---- Model weights (hundreds of GB) ------------------------------------------
models/
*.gguf
*.safetensors
*.bin

# ---- Vendored binaries -------------------------------------------------------
bin/
bin-b9771/
bin-b10182/
bin-poolside/
*.exe
*.dll

# ---- Dependency trees --------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/

# ---- Eval + bench outputs (regenerate them; do not diff them) ----------------
evals/results-tools.jsonl
evals/results-tools.csv
evals/results-code.jsonl
evals/percase-tools-*.csv
coding-eval/out/
evals/code/results-code.jsonl
evals/code/transcripts/
evals/code/transcripts-*/
evals/code/_work*/
evals/code/_smoke/
evals/code/_verify/
*.pre-fix-*.bak
*.pre-turnscope.bak

# ---- Raw benchmark dumps -----------------------------------------------------
vbench_*.txt
vbench_*.err
bench-*.txt
bench-*.err
bench-big.csv
sweep-*.csv
spec_*.out
spec_*.err
*-proof.txt
specproof-console.txt
longctx/

# ---- Logs / scratch ----------------------------------------------------------
# NB: *.log and *.err alone are NOT enough -- llama-server's stdout redirect is *.out,
# and the bench harness writes *.raw.txt model dumps. Both are per-run working files.
*.log
*.err
*.out
*.raw.txt
logs/
server.log
keep-resident.log
_prompt.txt

# ---- Bench result data -------------------------------------------------------
# Same rule as the other eval outputs: regenerate, do not diff. The FINDINGS belong in
# docs/BENCHMARKS.md, where they are explained; the raw JSONL is a working artifact.
evals/results/

# ---- Secrets / local config --------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here should ever be committed. Credentials belong in a secret manager,
# never in a file in this tree -- not even in a permissions allowlist.
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
secrets.*
**/settings.local.json
